<main><section class="case-hero section-shell reveal"><div><span class="eyebrow">Case study 02 · Finance data product</span><h1>Improving the business logic behind cash forecasting.</h1><p>Finance-domain co-ownership of Group Cash Flow Forecasting with Data Science, supporting multi-country and multi-currency liquidity planning.</p></div><div class="case-facts"><div><span>Role</span><strong>Finance Data Analytics Specialist</strong></div><div><span>Partners</span><strong>Data Science · Treasury · Finance</strong></div><div><span>Scope</span><strong>Global outstations · multiple currencies</strong></div><div><span>Platform</span><strong>Dataiku + enterprise finance data</strong></div></div></section><section class="case-body section-shell"><aside class="case-nav"><a href="#context">Context</a><a href="#logic-change">Concrete change</a><a href="#illustration">Illustration</a><a href="#ownership">Ownership</a><a href="#reflection">Takeaway</a><a class="back-link" href="/#work">← Back to work</a></aside><div><section class="case-section" id="context"><span class="eyebrow">Context</span><h2>A forecast is only as useful as the business logic behind it.</h2><p>Group Cash Flow Forecasting combines enterprise Finance data, model logic and Treasury use cases across countries and currencies. My role sits on the Finance side of the product: investigate variances, challenge assumptions and translate recurring business behaviour into changes the model can use.</p></section><section class="case-section" id="logic-change"><span class="eyebrow">Concrete change</span><h2>One production rule, improved.</h2><p>A payroll forecast used the earliest observed payment date. The code was deterministic; the business assumption was the problem. I tested whether recurrence was a better signal and changed the rule to use frequency-based date selection.</p><table class="decision-table"><thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rule</td><td>Choose the earliest observed payroll date.</td><td>Choose the date that recurs most frequently.</td></tr><tr><td>Failure mode</td><td>A one-off early payment could pull the forecast forward.</td><td>The rule follows recurring payroll behaviour rather than the earliest exception.</td></tr><tr><td>Where it lives</td><td colspan="2">Production business logic in Group Cash Flow Forecasting / Dataiku.</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td colspan="2">Shipped to production. No public quantified impact claim is made yet.</td></tr></tbody></table></section><PayrollRuleIllustration /><section class="case-section" id="ownership"><span class="eyebrow">Ownership</span><h2>Finance-domain co-owner, not solo model owner.</h2><div class="ownership-box"><div><h3>I own / contribute</h3><ul><li>Finance-domain investigations</li><li>Business-logic analysis</li><li>Targeted production enhancements</li><li>Translation between Finance, Treasury and Data Science</li></ul></div><div><h3>Shared / specialist ownership</h3><ul><li>Broader Data Science model</li><li>Model architecture and specialist data-science decisions</li><li>Enterprise platform engineering</li></ul></div></div></section><section class="case-section" id="reflection"><span class="eyebrow">Takeaway</span><h2>Technical sophistication does not replace a good business rule.</h2><p>The useful part of my role is the boundary: understand how Finance actually behaves, then make that logic explicit enough to test and improve.</p></section><section class="case-cta"><span class="eyebrow">Next</span><h2>Want to talk this through?</h2><div class="closing-actions"><a class="button primary" href="mailto:felipe.s.alves7@gmail.com">Email Felipe</a><a class="button ghost" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/felipealves7" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">LinkedIn ↗</a><a class="button ghost" href="/#work">More selected work</a></div></section></div></section></main>
</Base>
